00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.500
Good morning everyone.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:22.500
So today we're going to look at an important creative activity and that's hand knitting.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Ancient knitted garments have been found in many different countries,

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.000
showing that knitting is a global activity with a long history.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:41.000
When someone says the word knitting, we might well picture an elderly person,

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:50.000
a grandmother perhaps, sitting by the fire knitting garments for themselves or other members of the family.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:57.500
It's a homely image but one that may lead you to feel that knitting is an activity of the past

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:07.500
and indeed during the previous decade it was one of the skills that was predicted to vanish from everyday life.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:13.500
For although humans have sewn and knitted their own clothing for a very long time,

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:21.000
many of these craft-based skills went into decline when industrial machines took over,

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:28.000
mainly because they were no longer passed down from one generation to another.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:38.000
However, that's all changing and interest in knitting classes in many countries is actually rising,

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:44.500
as more and more people are seeking formal instruction in the skill.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:52.500
With that trend, we're also seeing an increase in the sales figures for knitting equipment.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:01.500
So why do people want to be taught to knit at a time when a machine can readily do the job for them?

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:10.000
The answer is that knitting as a handicraft has numerous benefits for those doing it.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.000
Let's consider what some of these might be.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:21.000
While many people knitted garments in the past because they couldn't afford to buy clothes,

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:29.000
it's still true today that knitting can be helpful if you're experiencing economic hardship.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:38.000
If you have several children who all need warm winter clothes, knitting may save you a lot of money.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:44.000
And the results of knitting your own clothes can be very rewarding,

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:53.000
even though the skills you need to get going are really quite basic and the financial outlay is minimal.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.000
But the more significant benefits in today's world are to do with wellbeing.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.000
In a world where it's estimated that we spend up to nine hours a day online,

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:13.000
doing something with our hands that is craft-based makes us feel good.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:19.000
It releases us from the stress of a technological fast-paced life.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:29.000
Now let's look back a bit to early knitting activities.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:34.000
In fact, no one really knows when knitting first began,

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:42.000
but archaeological remains have disclosed plenty of information for us to think about.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:50.000
One of the interesting things about knitting is that the earliest pieces of clothing that have been found

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:57.000
suggest that most of the items produced were round rather than flat.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:06.000
Discoveries from the 3rd and 4th centuries in Egypt show that things like socks and gloves

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:15.000
that were needed to keep hands and feet warm were knitted in one piece using four or five needles.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:22.000
That's very different from most knitting patterns today, which only require two.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:29.000
What's more, the very first needles people used were hand-carved out of wood

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:38.000
and other natural materials like bone, whereas today's needles are largely made of steel or plastic

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:44.000
and make that characteristic clicking sound when someone's using them.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:52.000
Ancient people knitted using yarns made from linen, hemp, cotton and wool

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.000
and these were often very rough on the skin.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:04.000
The spinning wheel, which allowed people to make finer yarns and produce much greater quantities of them,

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:11.000
led to the dominance of wool in the knitting industry, often favoured for its warmth.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:19.000
Another interesting fact about knitting is that because it was practised in so many parts of the world

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:26.000
for so many purposes, regional differences in style developed.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:34.000
This visual identity has allowed researchers to match bits of knitted clothing that have been unearthed

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:42.000
over time to the region from which the wearer came or the job that he or she did.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:49.000
As I've mentioned, knitting offered people from poor communities a way of making extra money

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.000
while doing other tasks.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:07:01.000
For many centuries, it seems, men, women and children took every opportunity to knit, for example,

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:08.000
while watching over sheep, walking to market or riding in boats.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.000
So, let's move on to take a look at...

